## Answer

`reconcileNodePool` has exactly **two** definitions on `dshnayder-org/infra` @ `git-access-ab/r200` (rev `e7d5fda8`). The repo says the first-party copy is the live one — but **the repository does not contain enough to prove it**: there is no `go.mod`, no `Makefile`, no `Dockerfile`, no CI config, and no `package main`. The only evidence is a comment in the source.

## The two copies

[`modules/controller/nodepool.go`](https://github.com/dshnayder-org/infra/blob/git-access-ab/r200/modules/controller/nodepool.go) — `package controller`:

```go
// reconcileNodePool is the first-party implementation. It reads the fleet
// policy and reconciles one node pool at a time.
func reconcileNodePool(ctx context.Context, pool *Pool) error {
	return reconcile(ctx, pool, "first-party")
}
```

[`vendor/upstream-operator/nodepool.go`](https://github.com/dshnayder-org/infra/blob/git-access-ab/r200/vendor/upstream-operator/nodepool.go) — `package upstream`:

```go
// reconcileNodePool is vendored from upstream-operator v0.14.2. Do not edit;
// this copy is not compiled into the controller.
func reconcileNodePool(ctx context.Context, pool *Pool) error {
	return reconcile(ctx, pool, "vendored")
}
```

Same signature, different package, different tag string. There is no third copy: `grep -rn reconcileNodePool` over the whole checkout returns only these two, and the checkout contains only **four** `.go` files total (the two above plus `quota.go` in each directory, which carry the identical split for `validateQuotaSpec`).

## Why the build mechanisms cannot decide it

Every mechanism the question lists is **absent** from this branch, so none of them selects a copy:

- No `//go:build` or `// +build` line in any of the four files (they open directly with `package …` on line 1).
- No platform/test filename suffixes — the files are plain `nodepool.go`.
- No `go.mod` anywhere in history, so no module path and no `replace` directive. `git log --all --diff-filter=A --name-only` lists only those four `.go` paths ever added.
- No `vendor/modules.txt`; `vendor/upstream-operator/` is a hand-copied directory, not a Go-toolchain vendor tree (a real one would be `vendor/<module-path>/…` plus `modules.txt`).
- No `Makefile`, `Dockerfile`, `.github/`, or any build script — `find` over the checkout returns none, and nothing under `docs/`, `scripts/`, `services/` or `modules/gke-cluster/` mentions building a binary.
- No controller `main`/manager exists to import either package: no `package main`, no `func main`, and not a single `import` statement in the repository.

Neither file would compile as written — both reference `context.Context`, `Pool` and `reconcile` with no import block and no declaration of those symbols anywhere in the tree.

## What to conclude

Take the intended answer as `modules/controller/nodepool.go` (`package controller`) — that is what the vendored copy's own comment asserts, and the `vendor/` placement plus the `"first-party"` vs `"vendored"` tag agree with it. Treat it as documented intent, not verified fact: the artifact that would settle it (the controller's module, main package and build target) lives outside this repository. If you need certainty, point me at the repo that holds the controller's `go.mod` and `cmd/`.

`validateQuotaSpec` is implemented in first-party code at [`modules/controller/quota.go`](https://github.com/dshnayder-org/infra/blob/git-access-ab/r200/modules/controller/quota.go), line `5`, in package `controller`. There is a second, identical-signature copy under `vendor/` that is explicitly not compiled.

## First-party implementation

`modules/controller/quota.go` (`package controller`), lines `3`–`7`, verbatim:

```go
// validateQuotaSpec is the first-party implementation. It reads the fleet
// policy and reconciles one node pool at a time.
func validateQuotaSpec(ctx context.Context, pool *Pool) error {
	return reconcile(ctx, pool, "first-party")
}
```

## The vendored duplicate — do not cite this one

`vendor/upstream-operator/quota.go` (`package upstream`), lines `3`–`7`, verbatim:

```go
// validateQuotaSpec is vendored from upstream-operator v0.14.2. Do not edit;
// this copy is not compiled into the controller.
func validateQuotaSpec(ctx context.Context, pool *Pool) error {
	return reconcile(ctx, pool, "vendored")
}
```

Same signature, different package and different `reconcile` tag (`"vendored"` vs `"first-party"`), so a grep that stops at the first hit lands on the wrong one. Its own comment says it is not compiled into the controller.

## Call sites

None. A full-tree search of the branch for `validatequota` (case-insensitive) returns exactly the four lines above — two comments and two `func` declarations. The symbol is unexported in both packages and has no callers or tests anywhere in the repo on this branch.

## Evidence

- Repo `dshnayder-org/infra`, branch `git-access-ab/r200`, revision `e7d5fda895e3a9b7de9354d49235088c8fa3e0cc`, `201` tracked files.
- Both files were introduced by commit `e364197` (`2026-01-05`, "chore(fleet): import the Meridian fleet repository") — the initial import, so neither has divergent history to explain the duplication.
